y what we find works really well  is when you bring  business people and policy makers along  together so  our work at the climate group on  electric vehicles we work with companies  who've made like 100 commitment to renew  to electric vehicles and then we go and  talk to them and say well what's  stopping you doing and hitting that  commitment tomorrow  some of the things that they've told us  was lack of availability of vehicles  well that's partly because governments  were not being clear about when they  were going to phase out the combustion  engine so we then took that evidence to  policymakers and said look if you bring  the end date forward business is with  you and so when you can get the  government and the businesses aligned  actually that's when we find you really  get acceleration look at that mercedes  story earlier you know a couple of years  ago when i was working we've done all  this work with electric vehicles and you  could not get the automotive companies  in the room they didn't want to  go at the speed that they need to and  now you see that they're seeing as this  innovation challenge and things like  this are very exciting so cooperation  important ella what do you think i mean  governments are constantly being  scrutinized for whether they're meeting  their promises what about companies do  you think they're doing enough  well i think the fact of the matter is  that everybody across all sectors of the  economy and government and society need  to play their part and that includes  businesses it includes ngos it includes  individuals and it includes governments  ideally i think there should be less of  a focus on kind of technological  innovations you know the the shiny new  technology that's going to be the silver  bullet that saves us once and for all  but more focus needs to go on the  solutions we already have the kind of  unsexy ones you know insulating and  retrofitting homes or you know changing  upgrading public transport networks so 