g and actually  things have really really moved on i  think now when people think about  climate change visually they probably  land more on on wildfires images like  that and i think some of the things that  have shifted have been around the public  discourse particularly the  student strikes and the school kids  saying what are you actually doing about  climate change and asking to hear more  about solutions and so i think we hear a  lot more now  less about prove to me this is happening  and more about what are you doing about  it so i think this can really trigger  public interest we see that with you  know david attenborough's programs and  people being really interested in in the  natural world but i think what people  will also want to know is okay how does  this relate back to me what's happening  about climate so the more we can talk  about why  this is necessary to advance our  understanding of climate as opposed to  you know we're just showing you more  sort of tragic pictures i think that's  really critical  ella do you think that's right you're  nodding there i can see that because it  is you know it's a frightening thing for  people to hear isn't it when they hear  about a big glacier like this  potentially melting and sea levels  rising so communication is very  important i mean absolutely as a climate  scientist who deals in these sorts of  statistics as my day job i can  very fully sympathize with the kind of  fear and  discomfort that comes with hearing about  all of these dramatic and potentially  catastrophic changes to our environment  but i think communicating that there is  a solution that there are things that we  can do and that there are things that  are being done um is really important as  well so it's not all doom and gloom the  whole time because that is just the  potential and that's what we're fighting  against well that's right and helen of  course you work with governments and  companies all the time to help encourage  them to go green so do you feel that the  fear factor if you like of issues like  this turn people off or does it  galvanize people to make change quicker  i think it's a bit of both i think you  know i talked about the school strikes  earlier and i think we saw that make a  big difference in businesses where  people were literally going home and  being asked by their own children what  are you doing about that and you know  what's your role in this and i think we  really saw that make a difference and i  think things like this will uh will  continue to fuel that and give people  that inspiration  to do more  okay let's move on to our next topic  shall we because back in november the uk  hosted the cop 26 climate conference now  the extent of the summit's success or  failure is still being debated but one  thing that most people agree on is that  governments can't hit climate targets  without the help of corporations now  prince charles wrote this week of a  military style campaign to marshal the  strength of the global private sector  because it will take trillions not  billions to transform our current fossil  fuel-based economy to one that is  genuinely renewable and sustainable  so how should companies be going green  well helen you work with companies all  the time to help them go green what role  do you think the corporations as opposed  to government should play and do we need  this military style campaign  to marshal their strength as prince  charles is calling for  look he's right about the scale and the  speed and the urgency so i completely  agree on all of that and the amount of  capital that needs to be deployed i  don't really like the idea of this  military campaign i think that um people  do more and do better when they're  incentivized to do things and when  they're pushed and the idea of kind of  that you know we're taking that approach  i don't think is one that will work i  think we've seen the the private sector  do a lot over the last few years and  actually what we find works really well  is when you bring  business people and policy makers along  together so  our work at the climate group on  electric vehicles we work with companies  who've made like 100 commitment to renew  to electric vehicles and then we go and  talk to them and say well what's  stopping you doing and hitting that  commitment tomorrow  some of the things that they've told us  was lack of availability of vehicles  well that's partly because governments  were not being clear about when they  were going to phase out the combustion  engine so we then took that evidence to  policymakers and said look if you bring  the end date forward business is with  you and so when you can get the  government and the businesses aligned  actually that's when we find you really  get acceleration look at that mercedes  story earlier you know a couple of years  ago when i was working we've done all  this work with electric vehicles and you  could not get the automotive companies  in the room they didn't want to  go at the speed that they need to and  now you see that they're seeing as this  innovation challenge and things like  this are very exciting so cooperation  important ella what do you think i mean  governments are constantly being  scrutinized for whether they're meeting  their promises what about companies do  you think they're doing enough  well i think the fact of the matter is  that everybody across all sectors of the  economy and government and society need  to play their part and that includes  businesses it includes ngos it includes  individuals and it includes governments  ideally i think there should be less of  a focus on kind of technological  innovations you know the the shiny new  technology that's going to be the silver  bullet that saves us once and for all  but more focus needs to go on the  solutions we already have the kind of  unsexy ones you know insulating and  retrofitting homes or you know changing  upgrading public transport networks so  that they work for people and they  actually want to use them you know those  sorts of changes that are kind of  less easy to sell and less easy to  say that you as a company are getting  involved in rather than focusing so much  on the latest technological innovation i  think i would really like to see more  partnerships between different sectors  so whether that's government and  businesses teaming up to actually push  this forwards on all fronts but i think  you know any money that's being spent on  tackling the climate crisis is always  good  okay on that note we will leave it but  dr ella gilbert and helen clarkson thank  you both very much indeed nice to see  you both  so that's it from us for today tomorrow  i'm going to be joined by specialist in  global environmental politics at cardiff  university jen allen and the director of  anti-poverty charity warren want assad  raymond  thanks for watching see you next time  [Music]  you
 

